1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,166
the attachment theory argues that a strong

2
00:00:03,166 --> 00:00:04,900
emotional and physical bond

3
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:08,433
to one primary caregiver in our first years of life

4
00:00:08,433 --> 00:00:10,533
is critical to our development

5
00:00:10,900 --> 00:00:14,266
if our bonding is strong and we are securely attached

6
00:00:14,266 --> 00:00:16,966
then we feel safe to explore the world

7
00:00:17,166 --> 00:00:20,033
we know that there is always that safe base

8
00:00:20,033 --> 00:00:22,200
which we can return to anytime

9
00:00:22,366 --> 00:00:26,066
if our bond is weak we feel insecurely attached

10
00:00:26,066 --> 00:00:27,500
we are afraid to leave

11
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:30,600
or explore a rather scary looking world

12
00:00:30,700 --> 00:00:33,100
because we are not sure if we can return

13
00:00:35,366 --> 00:00:37,266
people who are securely attached

14
00:00:37,266 --> 00:00:39,266
are said to have greater trust

15
00:00:39,366 --> 00:00:40,800
can connect to others

16
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:43,766
and as a result are more successful in life

17
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:47,733
insecurely attached people tend to mistrust others

18
00:00:47,866 --> 00:00:49,266
lack social skills

19
00:00:49,266 --> 00:00:51,866
and have problems forming relationships

20
00:00:52,100 --> 00:00:54,633
there is one type of secure attachment

21
00:00:54,633 --> 00:00:57,733
and there are three types of insecure attachments

22
00:00:58,033 --> 00:00:59,766
anxious ambivalent

23
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,000
anxious avoidant and anxious disorganized

24
00:01:04,366 --> 00:01:06,400
in responses to distress

25
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:08,866
the first three react organized

26
00:01:08,866 --> 00:01:11,533
while the last acts disorganized

27
00:01:13,966 --> 00:01:16,066
to understand the theory better

28
00:01:16,066 --> 00:01:18,066
let's look at Mr and Mrs

29
00:01:18,066 --> 00:01:21,766
Smith who have four children Luca Ann

30
00:01:21,866 --> 00:01:23,500
Joe and Amy

31
00:01:23,833 --> 00:01:26,766
the Smiths are lovely parents who cuddle

32
00:01:26,766 --> 00:01:28,666
make frequent eye contact

33
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:32,066
speak warmly and are always there for their kids

34
00:01:32,333 --> 00:01:33,700
but one day Mr

35
00:01:33,700 --> 00:01:36,133
Smith falls very sick and dies

36
00:01:36,566 --> 00:01:37,700
for Mrs Smith

37
00:01:37,700 --> 00:01:40,166
life now becomes very difficult

38
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:42,266
she spends all day working

39
00:01:42,266 --> 00:01:45,300
while at the same time trying to care for her children

40
00:01:45,566 --> 00:01:49,400
an impossible task at six years of age

41
00:01:49,433 --> 00:01:52,266
Luca's brain is for the most part developed

42
00:01:52,300 --> 00:01:55,466
his character strong and his world view shaped

43
00:01:55,666 --> 00:01:58,633
the new situation does not affect him much

44
00:01:58,633 --> 00:02:01,033
he knows there is still always mum

45
00:02:01,033 --> 00:02:04,533
his safe haven he feels securely attached

46
00:02:04,700 --> 00:02:08,766
later he turns into a trusting and optimistic young man

47
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:10,966
his self image is positive

48
00:02:13,233 --> 00:02:14,833
Anne who is three

49
00:02:14,833 --> 00:02:17,933
has problems coping with the new lack of attention

50
00:02:18,100 --> 00:02:21,333
to Anne her mother now acts unpredictably

51
00:02:21,500 --> 00:02:24,866
she is anxious about their relationship and as a result

52
00:02:24,866 --> 00:02:28,100
becomes clingy to get her mum's attention

53
00:02:28,100 --> 00:02:31,500
she has to raise her emotional state and scream

54
00:02:31,900 --> 00:02:35,366
when her mum finally reacts with a predictable response

55
00:02:35,366 --> 00:02:37,433
she herself acts ambivalent

56
00:02:37,433 --> 00:02:39,566
and doesn't show her true feelings

57
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:41,033
later in life

58
00:02:41,033 --> 00:02:44,166
others think Anne is unpredictable or Moody

59
00:02:44,300 --> 00:02:46,700
her self image is less positive

60
00:02:46,900 --> 00:02:50,066
her attachment style is anxious ambivalent

61
00:02:51,300 --> 00:02:54,766
two year old Joe spends his days with his uncle

62
00:02:54,766 --> 00:02:55,766
who loves him

63
00:02:55,766 --> 00:02:59,066
but thinks that a good education means being strict

64
00:02:59,300 --> 00:03:03,100
if Little Joe shows too much emotion or is too loud

65
00:03:03,166 --> 00:03:06,566
his uncle gets angry and sometimes punitive

66
00:03:06,833 --> 00:03:10,433
this scares Joe he learns that to avoid fear

67
00:03:10,433 --> 00:03:12,933
he has to avoid showing his feelings

68
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:14,733
also in other situations

69
00:03:15,333 --> 00:03:17,966
as an adult he continues this strategy

70
00:03:17,966 --> 00:03:20,666
and has problems entering relationships

71
00:03:20,700 --> 00:03:23,533
his image of himself is rather negative

72
00:03:23,800 --> 00:03:26,600
his attachment is anxious avoidant

73
00:03:28,366 --> 00:03:30,966
Amy who is just one year old

74
00:03:30,966 --> 00:03:32,766
gets sent to a nursery

75
00:03:32,866 --> 00:03:35,000
the staff there is poorly trained

76
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:37,666
overworked and often very stressed

77
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:40,000
some are outright abusive

78
00:03:40,366 --> 00:03:41,233
Amy therefore

79
00:03:41,233 --> 00:03:43,400
becomes anxious of the very people

80
00:03:43,400 --> 00:03:46,200
she seeks security from a conflict

81
00:03:46,200 --> 00:03:49,300
which totally disorganizes her ideas about love

82
00:03:49,300 --> 00:03:50,400
and safety

83
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:54,133
as she is experiencing fear without resolution

84
00:03:54,166 --> 00:03:57,100
she tries to avoid all social situations

85
00:03:57,600 --> 00:04:01,533
as an adult she thinks of herself as unworthy of love

86
00:04:01,666 --> 00:04:04,133
her self image is very negative

87
00:04:04,433 --> 00:04:07,566
her attachment is anxious disorganized

88
00:04:08,266 --> 00:04:09,166
our attachment

89
00:04:09,166 --> 00:04:12,333
is formed in the very first years of our lives

90
00:04:12,466 --> 00:04:16,133
a time when we are too young to communicate our anxiety

91
00:04:16,166 --> 00:04:19,800
and as a result can experience high levels of stress

92
00:04:20,300 --> 00:04:22,000
then our adrenal gland

93
00:04:22,033 --> 00:04:24,366
an organ sitting on top of our kidneys

94
00:04:24,433 --> 00:04:28,133
produces the stress hormones adrenaline and cortisol

95
00:04:28,466 --> 00:04:31,900
the heart rate increases the blood pressure goes up

96
00:04:31,900 --> 00:04:35,466
and we become alert if that happens frequently

97
00:04:35,466 --> 00:04:37,533
it is called toxic stress

98
00:04:37,966 --> 00:04:38,666
toxic

99
00:04:38,666 --> 00:04:41,900
because it impairs the development of a child's brain

100
00:04:41,900 --> 00:04:43,933
and weakens the immune system

101
00:04:44,233 --> 00:04:47,000
in embryos or at a very young age

102
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:50,766
toxic stress can even switch the expressions of genes

103
00:04:50,866 --> 00:04:54,100
which can affect our health many decades later

104
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:58,700
by simulating a strange situation

105
00:04:58,700 --> 00:05:00,966
we can assess an attachment style

106
00:05:00,966 --> 00:05:02,966
already by the age of one

107
00:05:03,166 --> 00:05:06,000
to do this we let the child play with their mother

108
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:08,200
for a few minutes inside a room

109
00:05:08,366 --> 00:05:10,400
then the child is left alone

110
00:05:10,500 --> 00:05:12,900
the key moment is the child's reaction

111
00:05:12,900 --> 00:05:15,966
when her mother returns securely attached

112
00:05:15,966 --> 00:05:18,533
children first usually hug their mother

113
00:05:18,600 --> 00:05:21,866
then can calm down and eventually get back to playing

114
00:05:22,200 --> 00:05:25,000
insecurely attached children can be ambivalent

115
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:26,066
and avoidant

116
00:05:26,433 --> 00:05:30,133
some can't stop crying or refuse to continue playing

117
00:05:32,433 --> 00:05:34,633
the long term effects of our attachment

118
00:05:34,633 --> 00:05:37,466
in the early years are well documented

119
00:05:37,766 --> 00:05:41,600
using the theory researchers at Minnesota University

120
00:05:41,600 --> 00:05:44,433
were able to predict already at age 3

121
00:05:44,433 --> 00:05:46,833
if a child would drop out of high school

122
00:05:46,833 --> 00:05:50,800
with 77% accuracy in another study

123
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:52,366
undergraduates at Harvard

124
00:05:52,366 --> 00:05:53,233
were asked to assess

125
00:05:53,233 --> 00:05:55,400
how close they felt to their parents

126
00:05:55,833 --> 00:05:57,400
thirty five years later

127
00:05:57,400 --> 00:05:59,166
they were asked about their health

128
00:05:59,600 --> 00:06:01,100
ninety one percent of those

129
00:06:01,100 --> 00:06:02,300
who said they had a rather

130
00:06:02,300 --> 00:06:04,566
broken relationship with their mother

131
00:06:04,600 --> 00:06:07,100
were also diagnosed with health issues

132
00:06:07,366 --> 00:06:09,533
including coronary artery disease

133
00:06:09,666 --> 00:06:12,133
hypertension and alcoholism

134
00:06:12,466 --> 00:06:15,300
for those that had reported a warm relationship

135
00:06:15,300 --> 00:06:19,600
the figure of poor health diagnosis was just 45%

136
00:06:21,500 --> 00:06:22,866
but there is another reason

137
00:06:22,866 --> 00:06:25,733
why the early years deserve special attention

138
00:06:26,033 --> 00:06:29,166
they are the starting place for subsequent behaviours

139
00:06:29,700 --> 00:06:32,866
a kid that feels securely attached at age two

140
00:06:32,866 --> 00:06:34,933
can make friends at kindergarten

141
00:06:35,300 --> 00:06:39,166
their world view gets reinforced with every interaction

142
00:06:39,166 --> 00:06:42,200
and they develop optimism as a result

143
00:06:42,200 --> 00:06:44,500
they make good relationships at school

144
00:06:44,500 --> 00:06:46,966
then at college and later at work

145
00:06:47,100 --> 00:06:48,900
highly insecurely attached

146
00:06:48,900 --> 00:06:51,533
children can miss out on this opportunity

147
00:06:53,833 --> 00:06:58,000
Psychologist John Bowlby a pioneer in attachment theory

148
00:06:58,000 --> 00:06:59,300
allegedly said

149
00:06:59,300 --> 00:07:01,900
what cannot be communicated to the mother

150
00:07:01,900 --> 00:07:04,300
cannot be communicated to the self

151
00:07:04,566 --> 00:07:08,000
in other words those who feel insecurely attached

152
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:10,533
might not quite understand themselves

153
00:07:10,833 --> 00:07:13,800
to get to know who they are and what they feel

154
00:07:13,800 --> 00:07:16,666
they might have to go way back in time

